#!/usr/bin/env python3.11
"""check-ci-workflow-drift — reports a registered repo whose declared
`ci_workflows` (the project registry) differ from the pull-request workflow
files GitHub actually lists for it (CQ·570, issue_01M5VPQ9Z92C58R3MPGDNCA7JM).

WHY. CQ·569 (issue_01M5VPJEV6F0XDSJX64TY5MRAV) found five registered repos
declaring `ci_workflows: {<repo>: []}` though each had a real
`.github/workflows/ci.yml` — nothing noticed, so their pull requests were
never CI-captured. This is the standing check that catches the next one:
read-only, run daily, reporting drift rather than fixing it.

AUTHENTICATES AS THE INSTALL'S OWN GITHUB APP, the same App
`bin/ci-result-capture` already mints through (`github_app.installed_app()`)
— see `_load_ci_result_capture` below for exactly what is reused from it and
why this never mints a second credential path of its own.

WHAT COUNTS AS A REPO TO CHECK: every repo of every registry unit that is
`lifecycle: active` and not `private` — `projects.load`'s own filter,
mirrored here rather than called through `ci_result_capture.tracked_pairs`.
See `declared_rows`'s own docstring for why that function, despite doing
almost this same walk, cannot be reused for the walk itself: it would hide
exactly the bug CQ·569 found.

HOW RUNS ON PULL_REQUEST IS READ FROM GITHUB. There is no GitHub endpoint
that lists a repo's workflows by trigger event — `GET
.../actions/workflows` lists registered workflows with no trigger info, and
`GET .../actions/workflows/{id}/runs?event=pull_request` only reflects runs
that have already happened, which a correctly configured but rarely
triggered workflow might not have any of. This instead reads each workflow
file's own `on:` key directly (the Contents API, decoding its base64 body,
the same two-call shape `kb_audit.audit_access`'s own workflow-files surface
already uses against this org) and parses it with `yaml.safe_load` —
checking BOTH the string key `"on"` and the boolean key `True`, because
unquoted YAML 1.1 (what `safe_load` implements) reads a bare `on:` scalar
key as the boolean `True`, not the string `"on"` — the well known GitHub
Actions YAML gotcha.

Each workflow file is then classified into exactly one of three classes
(`_pull_request_trigger_class`), not a single yes/no: `"every_pr"` (a
`pull_request` trigger with neither `paths` nor `paths-ignore` — every pull
request runs it, including one that also carries `branches`,
`branches-ignore` or `types`, since none of those skip a pull request based
on what it changed), `"filtered_pr"` (a `pull_request` trigger carrying
either of those two keys — some pull requests never run it), or `"not_pr"`
(no `pull_request` trigger at all, including `pull_request_target` — a
different trigger this check is not asked to fold in, and including a
`schedule`- or `workflow_dispatch`-only workflow). A string or list `on:`
form names events only and carries no filter keys, so a `pull_request`
found there is always `"every_pr"`.

A registered repo's `declared` workflow list and GitHub's own files compare
like with like: an `"every_pr"` file is exactly what `declared` is meant to
list, a `"not_pr"` file (declared or not) is irrelevant to drift either way,
and a `"filtered_pr"` file (declared or not) is never drift — it gets its
own `path_filtered` finding instead, since requiring it on every pull
request would leave a pull request outside its path filter waiting on a
check that never runs (issue_01M5Z5EN8TP04QBFM2Z7X8MPSJ: the first
post-merge run after PR #1046, main e956dfc6f, 2026-10-27, read two false
drift rows this way — a repo declaring `ci.yml` whose schedule-only second
workflow GitHub does not expect declared at all, and a repo declaring
`ci.yml` whose second workflow's `pull_request` trigger was narrowed to one
path, which every pull request outside that path would otherwise wait on
forever). The same issue's first post-merge run after PR #1047, main
f1a280bbe, 2026-10-27, then read four DECLARED workflows as `path_filtered`
for a reason that was never real: each one's `pull_request` trigger was
only `{branches: [main]}`, and `main` is each of those repos' default
branch, so every pull request the check is about already ran it. `branches`
and `branches-ignore` were in that run's filter-key list by the earlier
brief's own error, not because either can skip a pull request based on
what it changed; dropping those four required workflows out of the drift
comparison on account of a key that cannot do that is why only `paths` and
`paths-ignore` count as a filter below.

REPORTING, NEVER FIXING. For a repo this run disagrees with the registry, it
prints one line to stdout (always — this is this check's own dry-run/stdout
mode, `--no-report` makes it the ONLY output) and posts one `kind: alert`
signal through `signal_client.post_alert` (the same surface
`bin/ci1-heartbeat` and `bin/check-kb-guards` — this platform's other
scheduled anomaly checks — already report through; `mcp__ops__signal_log` is
the MCP tool a live agent session calls, and this is a standalone launchd
process with no such session). `dedup_key` is scoped to the UTC day, so a
breach that persists logs once a day, not once a run, and a breach whose
own shape changes (a different declared or GitHub list) logs again under a
new key even within the same day.

FIVE FINDING KINDS, reported through the same `_report` path:

  - DRIFT: the declared list and GitHub's `"every_pr"`-classified list
    disagree — a declared name with no file on GitHub at all, or an
    undeclared `"every_pr"` file. A declared name whose file is `"not_pr"`
    or `"filtered_pr"` is never drift on its own.
  - PATH FILTERED: a `"filtered_pr"` workflow file, declared or not. Never
    drift, and never changes the exit code — reported so the declared list
    and GitHub's files stay visibly accounted for, one line per file naming
    whether it is declared.
  - NOT ON GITHUB: the repo is registered but is not one the owner's
    convivy-bosun installation covers — read from `GET
    /installation/repositories` with a metadata-only token (CQ·576), never
    from a scoped `contents: read` mint's own refusal; today's two
    local-only registered repos, with no remote yet, are exactly this case.
    Reported, never a failure, and never folds the rest of that owner's
    covered repos into "unchecked" — they are still minted for and checked.
    A repo this read believed covered that still answers a repo-level 404
    (renamed or deleted between the listing read and this pass's own
    request) is reported the same way, through `repo_listing`'s own 404
    handling.
  - UNCHECKED: the repo's owner has no convivy-bosun installation this
    pass (`GA.NotInstalled`), the owner's installation lookup or
    installation-repository listing could not be read at all (neither a
    confirmed installation nor a confirmed absence), or the `contents:
    read` mint — scoped to exactly the repos that listing confirmed
    covered — was itself refused for any other reason. Reported
    as unchecked, never folded into "matching". The alert line names "no
    convivy-bosun installation" only for the confirmed-no-installation case
    (`GA.NotInstalled`); every other mint failure's alert instead names the
    owner and the mint failure itself (round 2 of CQ·570 — a scoped-mint
    refusal for a repo the installation does not cover used to be reported
    with the no-installation sentence, sending the operator to install an
    App that was already installed; CQ·576 stops that refusal from
    happening at all, by never scoping a mint to an uncovered repo).
  - COULD NOT BE CHECKED: GitHub's own listing failed for a reason that is
    neither 200 nor 404 — a 403, a transient 5xx, a malformed response, a
    workflow file that failed to parse. Reported with its reason, through
    `_report`, same as the other kinds; never only logged to stderr, since
    a silent skip here is exactly the failure mode CQ·570 exists to end.

EXIT CODE. Non-zero (1) in three cases, 0 otherwise:

  1. The check itself could not run at all — the App key is unusable, the
     App JWT could not be minted, or the project registry could not be
     read.
  2. There was at least one repo to check (`rows`) and none of them got a
     real HTTP 200 listing of its workflow directory — every one came back
     "not on GitHub" or "could not be checked", or every owner's mint
     itself failed so none was even attempted, "unchecked" across the
     board. Neither proves anything about whether the mint still carries
     `contents: read` — a silent permission regression (the mint accepted,
     every listing call then refused with 403) must fail exactly this
     loudly, not read as a quiet, empty-looking pass.
  3. Any repo ended "unchecked" or "could not be checked" this pass for
     any reason OTHER than GitHub confirming its owner has no
     convivy-bosun installation at all (`GA.NotInstalled`): a failed or
     ambiguous installation lookup (neither a confirmed installation nor
     a confirmed absence — a 5xx on both the org and user lookup, say), a
     failed installation-repositories listing, a refused or failed
     contents mint (422 included), or a refused or failed workflow
     listing (403 included). A failed read fails the run; only a
     confirmed absence does not. Case 2 alone misses this: a run that
     read 4 of 34 repos has at least one 200 listing, so case 2 never
     triggers, even though 30 repos under installed owners went unchecked
     (the steward, 2026-10-27).

Never failed by a repo reported "registered but not on GitHub" (not in the
installation's own repository list, or a repo-level 404) or by an owner
GitHub confirms has no installation at all (`GA.NotInstalled`) — both are
reported as findings, never as a failure, under case 2 and case 3 alike. An
owner with no installation, or whose installation-repository listing or
contents mint GitHub refused, is never "attempted" in case 2's sense — it
is only ever reported unchecked. Drift and path-filtered findings never
change the exit code: a declared-versus-GitHub difference, or a
path-filtered file, is reported, never a reason to fail the run.

This is the inverse of `check-kb-guards`'s own exit-code table (which maps
a breach to 2) because the two checks answer different questions for their
callers: `check-kb-guards`'s caller treats a breach as the thing
`launchctl`'s own status should surface; here the schedule owns no gate a
drift or not-found finding would feed, and a non-zero "it found something"
would have launchd's own failure accounting (and anyone paging on it) treat
an expected, reportable finding as an operational fault. A run that checked
nothing, or whose repos went substantially unchecked for a reason other
than a confirmed-no-installation owner, is different: nobody should read
that as "nothing is wrong."

Usage:
    check-ci-workflow-drift [--no-report]
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import base64
import datetime as dt
import hashlib
import importlib.util
import sys
import urllib.request
from importlib.machinery import SourceFileLoader
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"))

import github_app as GA  # noqa: E402
import projects as PR  # noqa: E402
import signal_client as SC  # noqa: E402
import yaml  # noqa: E402

_APP_LABEL = "the install's GitHub App"

_WORKFLOW_FILE_SUFFIXES = (".yml", ".yaml")

# This script's own permission grant — distinct from `CRC._READ_ONLY_PERMISSIONS`,
# which `ci-result-capture` mints with and which has no `contents` scope because
# that script never reads a file's content. This one does (the Contents API,
# `repo_listing` below), so it mints its own token with its own grant rather
# than widen the shared constant for a script that does not need the scope.
_DRIFT_READ_PERMISSIONS = {"actions": "read", "contents": "read", "metadata": "read"}


def _load_ci_result_capture():
    """`bin/ci-result-capture` itself — this check reuses its
    `_READ_ONLY_PERMISSIONS`, its `GhTokenRefresher` class and its
    `_pair_owner` helper rather than re-deriving any of them, so there is
    exactly one place this platform mints a read token for the install's own
    GitHub App from. `bin/` CLIs are extension-less and not importable as a
    normal module — the same reason `bin/model-scorecard` loads
    `bin/agentctl` this way."""
    path = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "ci-result-capture"
    loader = SourceFileLoader("ci_result_capture", str(path))
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(
        importlib.util.spec_from_loader("ci_result_capture", loader))
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


CRC = _load_ci_result_capture()


# --- which repos to check, and what they declare ----------------------------


def declared_rows() -> tuple[dict, ...]:
    """One row per active, non-private registry unit's repo: `repo`
    (owner/repo, via `projects.repo_github_slug`), `product` (the unit's own
    slug) and `declared` (the unit's own `ci_workflows[repo]`, or the
    `ci.yml` default — `projects.ci_workflows`'s own rule).

    WALKS THE REGISTRY DIRECTLY, NOT THROUGH `ci_result_capture.tracked_pairs`,
    even though the two walks are otherwise the same (active, not private;
    `projects.repo_github_slug` for the owner) and `tracked_pairs` already
    exists: that function emits one entry per TRACKED workflow FILE, built by
    iterating `projects.ci_workflows(repo)` and appending one pair per name in
    it — a repo whose own `ci_workflows` field is `[]` iterates zero times and
    emits NO row there at all. That is the exact shape of the bug CQ·569
    found (five repos declaring `ci_workflows: {repo: []}`): a walk that
    skips a repo with nothing declared would skip precisely the repos this
    check exists to catch. This walk keeps every repo regardless of how many
    workflows it declares, including none.

    Raises whatever `projects.load()` raises when the registry cannot be
    read — the caller must refuse the run rather than check an empty or
    partial list (see `main()`)."""
    rows: list[dict] = []
    for slug, unit in PR.load().items():
        if unit.get("lifecycle") != "active" or unit.get("private") is True:
            continue
        for repo in unit.get("repos", []):
            full_repo = PR.repo_github_slug(repo, unit=unit)
            rows.append({"repo": full_repo, "product": slug,
                        "declared": tuple(PR.ci_workflows(repo))})
    return tuple(rows)


# --- per-owner token minting (reused from ci-result-capture) ----------------


def _owner_repos(rows: tuple[dict, ...]) -> dict[str, list[str]]:
    """Which bare repo names (no owner prefix) this pass will check,
    grouped by owner — the full candidate scope `mint_tokens` filters
    against the installation's own coverage (`_installation_repos`) before
    passing what survives to GitHub's `repositories=`. *rows* is already
    `declared_rows()`'s own output, so it already excludes a skipped unit's
    repo; an owner all of whose units were skipped has no entry here at
    all, and `mint_tokens` mints nothing for it. This is why a public and a
    `private: true` unit sharing one `github_owner` cannot both end up on
    the same token: the private one's repo was never in *rows* to begin
    with."""
    owner_repos: dict[str, set[str]] = {}
    for row in rows:
        owner = CRC._pair_owner(row)
        _, _, repo_name = row["repo"].partition("/")
        owner_repos.setdefault(owner, set()).add(repo_name)
    return {owner: sorted(names) for owner, names in owner_repos.items()}


# The grant `_installation_repos` mints with, to learn which of an owner's
# registered repos the installation actually covers — unscoped (no
# `repositories=`, since that is exactly what this call is finding out) and
# metadata-only, the same low-sensitivity grant this script held before
# #1045 added the owner-scoped `contents: read` mint below it. Kept
# distinct from `_DRIFT_READ_PERMISSIONS`: this token never reads a repo's
# contents, only the installation's own repository list.
_INSTALLATION_LIST_PERMISSIONS = {"metadata": "read"}


def _installation_repos(jwt: str, installation_id: str, *,
                        opener=urllib.request.urlopen) -> set[str]:
    """Every repo (`"owner/repo"`) the convivy-bosun App's installation
    *installation_id* actually covers — read through `GET
    /installation/repositories` with a metadata-only, unscoped token
    (`_INSTALLATION_LIST_PERMISSIONS`), paging through every page GitHub
    reports (`per_page=100`, continuing while a page still came back
    non-empty and the accumulated count is still under that page's own
    `total_count` — `GA.request` carries no response headers, so there is
    no `Link` header here to page by instead).

    Raises `GA.Unreadable` when the token cannot be minted or any page's
    listing call fails to answer 200 with a `repositories` list —
    `mint_tokens` catches this exactly like a failed
    `installation_for_owner` lookup, folding the whole owner into
    "unchecked" (CQ·576: this is a failed READ, never a confirmed absence,
    so it must never be read as "none of this owner's repos are
    covered")."""
    payload = GA.installation_token_payload(
        jwt, installation_id, permissions=_INSTALLATION_LIST_PERMISSIONS, opener=opener)
    token = payload["token"]
    names: set[str] = set()
    page = 1
    while True:
        code, listing = GA.request(
            "GET", f"{GA.API}/installation/repositories?per_page=100&page={page}",
            token, None, opener=opener)
        if code != 200 or not isinstance(listing, dict):
            raise GA.Unreadable(
                f"the installation's repository list could not be read ({code})")
        repos = listing.get("repositories")
        if not isinstance(repos, list):
            raise GA.Unreadable(
                "the installation's repository list response carried no repositories")
        for entry in repos:
            full_name = entry.get("full_name") if isinstance(entry, dict) else None
            if full_name:
                names.add(full_name)
        total_count = listing.get("total_count")
        if not repos or not isinstance(total_count, int) or len(names) >= total_count:
            break
        page += 1
    return names


def mint_tokens(jwt: str, owner_repos: dict[str, list[str]]):
    """For each owner in *owner_repos*: first learns which of its repos the
    convivy-bosun installation actually covers (`_installation_repos`,
    CQ·576), then mints one read-only `CRC.GhTokenRefresher` scoped to
    exactly the covered ones — the same per-owner-installation mint shape
    `ci_result_capture.main()` performs (`GA.installation_for_owner`,
    `CRC.GhTokenRefresher`), reused here rather than re-derived so this
    check authenticates through the same App and the same key
    `ci-result-capture` already does — never a second credential path. The
    grant differs: `_DRIFT_READ_PERMISSIONS`, not `CRC._READ_ONLY_PERMISSIONS`,
    because `repo_listing` reads file content through the Contents API and
    that needs `contents: read`, a scope `ci-result-capture` has no reason
    to hold.

    Each owner's contents-read token is ALSO scoped to the covered subset
    of `owner_repos[owner]` via `GhTokenRefresher`'s own `repositories=`
    (the same `installation_token_payload` parameter `github_app.mint` uses
    to scope a token to one repo). Without this, `contents: read` would let
    the token minted to check one repo under an owner read every OTHER
    repo that owner's installation can see, including one `declared_rows`
    deliberately excluded (a `private: true` unit sharing a `github_owner`
    with an active public one). A repo in `owner_repos[owner]` the
    installation does NOT cover is never placed in that `repositories=`
    list at all — GitHub refuses the WHOLE mint with 422 the moment one
    listed repo is not accessible to the installation (the production
    failure this fix answers, PR #1045's post-merge run on 2026-10-27),
    so leaving an uncovered repo out is what lets every covered repo under
    the same owner still be minted for and checked.

    The refresher itself, never a token string, is what this function
    stores and returns: `repo_listing` resolves each refresher's own
    current token immediately before every GitHub call it makes, through
    `CRC._resolve_gh_token` — never once here at mint time, since an
    installation token can expire partway through a run that reads many
    repos.

    Returns `(tokens, unchecked, not_found)`. `tokens` maps an owner to its
    minted refresher — absent for an owner none of whose repos the
    installation covers; no contents mint is attempted for one. `unchecked`
    maps an owner this pass could not learn coverage for, or could not mint
    a contents-read token for, to the `GA.Unreadable` raised while trying —
    `GA.NotInstalled` (a subclass of it; GitHub's own confirmation the App
    has no installation there) or any other `GA.Unreadable`. Keeping the
    raised exception rather than just the owner's name is what lets
    `finding_line` tell the confirmed-no-installation case from every other
    failure apart (round 2 of CQ·570) rather than reporting one sentence
    for both. `not_found` maps an owner to the bare names of its repos the
    installation's own listing confirmed it does NOT cover — CQ·576's own
    fix: these are reported `not_found` ("registered but not on GitHub"),
    never folded into `unchecked` and never the reason a contents mint for
    the REST of that owner's repos is skipped or fails."""
    tokens: dict[str, object] = {}
    unchecked: dict[str, GA.Unreadable] = {}
    not_found: dict[str, set[str]] = {}
    for owner in sorted(owner_repos):
        try:
            inst = GA.installation_for_owner(jwt, owner, label=_APP_LABEL)
            inst_id = str(inst.get("id") or "")
            if not inst_id:
                raise GA.Unreadable("the installation carries no id")
            covered = _installation_repos(jwt, inst_id)
        except GA.Unreadable as e:
            print(f"check-ci-workflow-drift: {owner}: the convivy-bosun installation "
                 f"could not be confirmed, or its repository list could not be read "
                 f"({e}); every {owner} repo is reported unchecked this pass",
                 file=sys.stderr)
            unchecked[owner] = e
            continue

        present = [name for name in owner_repos[owner] if f"{owner}/{name}" in covered]
        missing = [name for name in owner_repos[owner] if f"{owner}/{name}" not in covered]
        if missing:
            not_found[owner] = set(missing)
        if not present:
            continue
        try:
            refresher = CRC.GhTokenRefresher(jwt, inst_id,
                                             permissions=_DRIFT_READ_PERMISSIONS,
                                             repositories=present)
            refresher.token()
            tokens[owner] = refresher
        except GA.Unreadable as e:
            print(f"check-ci-workflow-drift: {owner}: convivy-bosun installation "
                 f"token could not be minted ({e}); {owner}'s covered repos are "
                 f"reported unchecked this pass", file=sys.stderr)
            unchecked[owner] = e
    return tokens, unchecked, not_found


# --- reading GitHub's own pull-request-triggered workflow list --------------


# The two `pull_request` sub-keys that narrow which pull requests a
# workflow runs on by what changed. A `pull_request` trigger carrying
# either of these is "filtered_pr", never "every_pr" — requiring it on
# every pull request would leave one outside the filter waiting on a
# check that never runs. `branches`/`branches-ignore` narrow by target
# branch, not by change, and every PR this check is about targets the
# repo's default branch, so they leave a trigger "every_pr".
_PR_FILTER_KEYS = ("paths", "paths-ignore")

EVERY_PR = "every_pr"
FILTERED_PR = "filtered_pr"
NOT_PR = "not_pr"


def _pull_request_trigger_class(doc) -> str:
    """Classifies a parsed workflow document's `on:` trigger into exactly
    one of `EVERY_PR`, `FILTERED_PR` or `NOT_PR` — never a plain yes/no,
    since a `pull_request` trigger narrowed by `_PR_FILTER_KEYS` answers a
    different question to a bare one. `pull_request_target` is `NOT_PR`,
    like any other non-`pull_request` trigger — a different trigger this
    check is not asked to fold in.

    Checks both the string key `"on"` and the boolean key `True`: PyYAML's
    `safe_load` (YAML 1.1) reads an unquoted `on:` scalar key as the boolean
    `True`, not the string `"on"` — the GitHub Actions YAML gotcha every
    workflow file is written against without knowing it. A string or list
    `on:` form names events only and carries no filter keys, so a
    `pull_request` found there is always `EVERY_PR`. A document missing
    `on` entirely, or whose value is none of a string/list/mapping, is
    `NOT_PR`."""
    if not isinstance(doc, dict):
        return NOT_PR
    on = doc.get("on", doc.get(True))
    if isinstance(on, str):
        return EVERY_PR if on == "pull_request" else NOT_PR
    if isinstance(on, list):
        return EVERY_PR if "pull_request" in on else NOT_PR
    if isinstance(on, dict):
        if "pull_request" not in on:
            return NOT_PR
        filters = on["pull_request"]
        if isinstance(filters, dict) and any(k in filters for k in _PR_FILTER_KEYS):
            return FILTERED_PR
        return EVERY_PR
    return NOT_PR


def repo_listing(full_repo: str, token, *, opener=urllib.request.urlopen):
    """GitHub's own answer for *full_repo*: `("ok", classified, listed)`
    where *classified* maps every workflow file's own name to its
    `_pull_request_trigger_class` (`EVERY_PR`, `FILTERED_PR` or `NOT_PR`) —
    never a bare name list, since the caller (`main()`) needs each file's
    class to compare like with like rather than fold a schedule-only or
    path-filtered file into the same bucket as one that runs on every pull
    request. `("not_found", None, False)` when GitHub answers 404 for the
    repo itself — the confirmed-absent case CQ·570 asks be reported, never
    failed, since two registered repos have no GitHub remote today — or
    `("error", reason, listed)` for any other failed read, which the caller
    now reports as its own "could not be checked" finding, through
    `_report`, rather than only logging it.

    *listed* is `True` only when the workflow-directory contents listing
    itself (the `GET .../contents/.github/workflows?ref=...` call below)
    answered HTTP 200 — never for a repo-level 404, an absent-directory 404,
    or a 403/5xx anywhere in the walk. This is the one signal `main()` uses
    to tell a real, permission-working read from a run that minted a token
    GitHub accepted but then refused every listing call: a confirmed-absent
    repo or workflow directory proves nothing about whether `contents: read`
    is still granted, so neither counts. A workflow FILE read failing after a
    200 directory listing still leaves *listed* `True` — the directory read
    that matters already succeeded.

    *token* is a plain token string or a `CRC.GhTokenRefresher` — either way,
    `CRC._resolve_gh_token(token)` is called again immediately before EVERY
    `GA.request` below, never once at the top of this function: an
    installation token can expire partway through a run that reads many
    repos' workflow files, and a token resolved once here and reused would
    repeat the exact 401-on-every-real-call bug `ci_result_capture.main()`
    measured on 2026-10-25 (see `CRC.GhTokenRefresher`'s own docstring).

    Reads the Contents API at the repo's own default branch, the same
    two-call shape (`GET /repos/{repo}` for `default_branch`, then `GET
    .../contents/.github/workflows?ref=<it>`) `kb_audit.audit_access`'s own
    workflow-files surface already uses against this org — never the `git`
    binary, never a clone."""
    bearer, _ = CRC._resolve_gh_token(token)
    code, payload = GA.request("GET", f"{GA.API}/repos/{full_repo}", bearer, None,
                               opener=opener)
    if code == 404:
        return "not_found", None, False
    default_branch = payload.get("default_branch") if code == 200 and isinstance(payload, dict) else None
    if code != 200 or not default_branch:
        return "error", f"repo could not be read ({code})", False

    bearer, _ = CRC._resolve_gh_token(token)
    code, payload = GA.request(
        "GET", f"{GA.API}/repos/{full_repo}/contents/.github/workflows?ref={default_branch}",
        bearer, None, opener=opener)
    if code == 404:
        return "ok", {}, False
    if code != 200 or not isinstance(payload, list):
        return "error", f"workflow directory could not be listed ({code})", False

    names = sorted(
        entry["name"] for entry in payload
        if isinstance(entry, dict) and entry.get("type") == "file"
        and str(entry.get("name", "")).endswith(_WORKFLOW_FILE_SUFFIXES))

    classified: dict[str, str] = {}
    for name in names:
        bearer, _ = CRC._resolve_gh_token(token)
        code, file_payload = GA.request(
            "GET", f"{GA.API}/repos/{full_repo}/contents/.github/workflows/{name}"
                  f"?ref={default_branch}",
            bearer, None, opener=opener)
        if code != 200 or not isinstance(file_payload, dict) or "content" not in file_payload:
            return "error", f"{name} could not be read ({code})", True
        try:
            text = base64.b64decode(file_payload["content"]).decode("utf-8", "replace")
            doc = yaml.safe_load(text)
        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — content this job did not author
            return "error", f"{name} could not be parsed ({type(e).__name__})", True
        classified[name] = _pull_request_trigger_class(doc)
    return "ok", classified, True


def _compare(declared: set[str] | tuple[str, ...], classified: dict[str, str]):
    """Compares *declared* against GitHub's own *classified* files (the
    `("ok", classified, ...)` `repo_listing` returns) like with like:
    returns `(github_list, filtered_names)`.

    `github_list` is the sorted list `main()` compares `sorted(declared)`
    against, and is also what a `drift` finding's own alert line shows —
    every `EVERY_PR` file (whether declared or not, so an undeclared one
    still surfaces as a mismatch), plus any declared name that does have a
    file on GitHub regardless of its class (so a declared `NOT_PR` or
    `FILTERED_PR` file reads as accounted for, never as drift, while a
    declared name with NO file on GitHub at all is left out and so still
    mismatches). A plain `sorted(declared) != github_list` is therefore
    drift exactly when some declared name has no file on GitHub at all, or
    some undeclared file is `EVERY_PR` — never for a declared or undeclared
    `FILTERED_PR` or `NOT_PR` file.

    `filtered_names` is the sorted list of every `FILTERED_PR` file,
    declared or not — `main()` reports each as its own `path_filtered`
    finding, never as drift."""
    github_list = sorted(
        name for name, cls in classified.items()
        if cls == EVERY_PR or name in declared)
    filtered_names = sorted(name for name, cls in classified.items() if cls == FILTERED_PR)
    return github_list, filtered_names


# --- reporting ---------------------------------------------------------------


def _utc_today() -> str:
    return dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc).date().isoformat()


def finding_line(row: dict, kind: str, detail, owner: str) -> str:
    """*detail* carries a different shape per *kind*: `drift`'s own
    sorted GitHub list (the `EVERY_PR` files, plus any declared file that
    is present under any class — what the comparison actually used),
    `path_filtered`'s own `{"file": name, "declared": bool}` mapping,
    `error`'s own reason string, the `GA.Unreadable` `mint_tokens` caught
    for `unchecked` (see below), or `None` for `not_found`.

    For `unchecked`, `detail` is the exception `mint_tokens` raised while
    minting that owner's token. Only `GA.NotInstalled` — GitHub's own
    confirmation the App has no installation on the owner — gets the "no
    convivy-bosun installation" sentence; every other `GA.Unreadable`
    (a failed or ambiguous installation lookup itself, the installation's
    own repository list could not be read, an installation with no id, or
    the contents-read mint itself refused) instead names the owner and
    `str(detail)`, the mint failure's own
    reason (round 2 of CQ·570 — the blanket "no installation" sentence was
    false for every one of those causes and sent the operator to install
    an App that was already installed, rather than to fix the registry
    entry or the installation's repo selection; CQ·576 additionally stops
    a repo the installation does not cover from ever reaching `unchecked`
    at all — that case is `not_found` instead). `str(detail)`
    never carries a token, JWT or key: every `GA.Unreadable` reachable here
    is built from an HTTP status code, an owner name, or GitHub's own
    `message` field on a REFUSED token-creation call, which by definition
    never echoes a token nobody was issued (see `mint_tokens`'s own
    docstring)."""
    declared = sorted(row["declared"])
    if kind == "drift":
        return f"{row['repo']}: declared {declared} != GitHub {detail}"
    if kind == "path_filtered":
        declared_word = "declared" if detail["declared"] else "undeclared"
        return (f"{row['repo']}: {detail['file']} is {declared_word} and runs on "
                f"pull_request with a path filter; never compared for drift")
    if kind == "not_found":
        return f"{row['repo']}: registered but not on GitHub (declared {declared})"
    if kind == "unchecked":
        if isinstance(detail, GA.NotInstalled):
            return (f"{row['repo']}: unchecked — no convivy-bosun installation for "
                    f"{owner!r}; a person must install the convivy-bosun App on "
                    f"{owner!r} (declared {declared})")
        return (f"{row['repo']}: unchecked — {owner!r}'s convivy-bosun installation "
                f"could not be used to read this repo this pass ({detail}) "
                f"(declared {declared})")
    if kind == "error":
        return f"{row['repo']}: could not be checked ({detail})"
    raise AssertionError(f"unknown finding kind: {kind!r}")


def _dedup_key(repo: str, kind: str, detail: str, day: str) -> str:
    digest = hashlib.sha256(detail.encode()).hexdigest()[:16]
    return f"ci-workflow-drift:{repo}:{kind}:{digest}:{day}"


def _report(row: dict, kind: str, detail, owner: str, day: str,
           *, dry_run: bool) -> None:
    line = finding_line(row, kind, detail, owner)
    print(line)
    if dry_run:
        return
    dedup_detail = f"{sorted(row['declared'])}|{detail}|{owner}"
    title = f"[ci-workflow-drift] {line}"
    body = (f"**Registry/GitHub CI-workflow drift.** {line}\n\n"
           f"The crew registry's own `ci_workflows` field for `{row['repo']}` "
           f"is what `bin/ci-result-capture` reads to know what to track; a "
           f"wrong value here means a pull request's CI silently stops being "
           f"captured (CQ·569).")
    SC.post_alert("ci-workflow-drift", SC.sanitise_body(f"{title}\n\n{body}"),
                 product=row["product"],
                 dedup_key=_dedup_key(row["repo"], kind, dedup_detail, day),
                 meta={"severity": "medium", "finding": kind})


# --- entry point --------------------------------------------------------------


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="check-ci-workflow-drift",
        description="Report a registered repo whose declared ci_workflows "
                    "differ from GitHub's own pull-request workflow list.")
    parser.add_argument("--no-report", action="store_true",
                        help="print findings to stdout; do not post alert "
                             "signals (dry run, safe to run by hand)")
    opts = parser.parse_args(argv)

    try:
        app = GA.installed_app()
    except GA.Unreadable as e:
        print(f"check-ci-workflow-drift: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    problem = GA.key_problem(app.key_path, label=_APP_LABEL)
    if problem:
        print(f"check-ci-workflow-drift: {problem}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    try:
        rows = declared_rows()
    except Exception as ex:
        print(f"check-ci-workflow-drift: the project registry could not be "
             f"read ({type(ex).__name__}: {ex}); refusing this run",
             file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    try:
        jwt = GA.app_jwt(app.key_path, app.app_id)
    except GA.Unreadable as e:
        print(f"check-ci-workflow-drift: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    tokens, unchecked_owners, not_found_owners = mint_tokens(jwt, _owner_repos(rows))

    day = _utc_today()
    findings = 0
    listed = 0
    # A repo that still ends "unchecked" or "could not be checked" for any
    # reason other than a confirmed `GA.NotInstalled` (exit-code case 3,
    # module docstring): a failed or ambiguous installation lookup, a
    # failed installation-repository listing, a 422 on the contents mint,
    # or a refused/failed workflow listing. `rows and not listed` alone
    # (case 2) misses this the moment even one other repo got a real 200
    # listing — the production shape that read 4 of 34 repos and exited 0
    # regardless (the steward, 2026-10-27). Never counts a `GA.NotInstalled`
    # owner or a `not_found` repo — neither is a failure, under either exit
    # case.
    blocking = 0
    for row in rows:
        owner = CRC._pair_owner(row)
        _, _, repo_name = row["repo"].partition("/")
        if repo_name in not_found_owners.get(owner, ()):
            _report(row, "not_found", None, owner, day, dry_run=opts.no_report)
            findings += 1
            continue
        if owner in unchecked_owners:
            detail = unchecked_owners[owner]
            _report(row, "unchecked", detail, owner, day, dry_run=opts.no_report)
            findings += 1
            if not isinstance(detail, GA.NotInstalled):
                blocking += 1
            continue
        status, detail, row_listed = repo_listing(row["repo"], tokens[owner])
        if row_listed:
            listed += 1
        if status == "not_found":
            _report(row, "not_found", None, owner, day, dry_run=opts.no_report)
            findings += 1
        elif status == "error":
            _report(row, "error", detail, owner, day, dry_run=opts.no_report)
            findings += 1
            blocking += 1
        else:
            declared = set(row["declared"])
            github_list, filtered_names = _compare(declared, detail)
            if sorted(row["declared"]) != github_list:
                _report(row, "drift", github_list, owner, day, dry_run=opts.no_report)
                findings += 1
            for name in filtered_names:
                _report(row, "path_filtered", {"file": name, "declared": name in declared},
                        owner, day, dry_run=opts.no_report)
                findings += 1

    print(f"check-ci-workflow-drift: {findings} finding(s) across {len(rows)} repo(s)")
    if rows and not listed:
        print("check-ci-workflow-drift: no repo's workflow directory could be "
             "listed this pass; this run proved nothing, refusing it",
             file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if blocking:
        print(f"check-ci-workflow-drift: {blocking} repo(s) ended unchecked "
             "or could not be checked this pass for a reason other than "
             "GitHub confirming the owner has no installation; refusing "
             "this run", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
